APPLE IIGS IMAGEWRITER PRINTER DRIVER 4.2 	--		TECHNICAL DESCRIPTION (ERS)Written by Ben KoningBased on ImageWriter LQ driver by Ben KoningPartially based on original ImageWriter LQ low-level driver by Jason HarperJune 1991GENERALThe ImageWriter printer driver, version 4.0, is a fully rewritten Apple IIgsprinter driver based on the new "4.0" printer driver for the ImageWriter LQ,thus taking advantage of the latter driver's numerous speed and featureenhancements.  It should be installed with the following pathname and type:*/SYSTEM/DRIVERS/IMAGEWRITER		type=$BB	auxtype=$0001This driver contains the new type of entry point (starting with the zerobyte), and uses pop-up menus and the improved Control Manager tool set.Therefore, it will only operate on System Disk 5.0 or later.A new call, PrGetPrinterSpecs, is supported, which checks to see if thedevice connected is really an ImageWriter and returns information regardingthe existence of a color or black/white ribbon.DIALOGS AND USER COMMUNICATIONExistence or nonexistence of the Cut Sheet Feeder and Color Ribbon optionsis manifested by enabled or disabled (dimmed) items in the Job Dialog, evenif printer over a direct-connect port driver; the printer is actually polledto check for these items.  WARNING: A new version of the various port drivers,which offer nonblocking I/O to direct-connect devices, should be available.Otherwise, if no printer is on-line, selecting the Job Dialog will cause thesystem to hang.  If a port driver which does support nonblocking I/O (such asAppleTalk) and the printer isn't on line, the Job Dialog call issues an alertand returns the portNotOn tool error code.Pop-up menus and the new control manager calls are used; better progressindicators showing the number of sheets left to print, printer port errormessages with priority, etc., have been implemented.  All text is locatedwithin one source code file for easy localization.If the Shift-Option-Apple keys are down while the PrStlDialog or PrJobDialogis being called, an about box will appear.If bit 15 of wDev is set in the print record (by an application), then thestatus message box usually displayed during the printing process is not shown.The cursor is still changed to WaitCursor/InitCursor, however, and the manualfeed dialog box is still shown if required.NEW DRAFT (TEXT) PRINTING FEATURESFor draft printing, a mini-spooling method (see below for the actual spoolingmethodology) has been implemented which allows multiple-copy draft-mode printingto work (most applications do not print more than one copy when requested indraft mode when the old ImageWriter II driver is selected).  Applications whichrepeat the printing loop in order to print the extra copies should set theoiCopies field of the print record to 1 before executing the printing loop(s).In addition, this methodology allows top-to-bottom printing, so that reversepaper feed (which isn't possible because of the way the Cut Sheet Feeder works)is not necessary.In draft printing, font attributes such as boldface, underline, italic, pointsize, and font name, as well as special international characters, are allconsidered dynamically while selecting an appropriate native ASCIIcharacters.  The code is optimized so that characters are sent to the printerin blocks.  These blocks are flushed when an international character is printedor when the end of a QuickDraw string is reached.  Thus, printing many smallstrings or many consecutive international characters will result in lowerspeed performance."SPOOL" (GRAPHICS) PRINTING FEATURESStandard-resolution graphics printing and 2X high resolution graphics printingare offered, with 50% vertical condense and 100/50% life size scalingoptions, in portrait or landscape orientations.  A fast mode which is Standardresolution without enabling boldface and unidirectional printing is alsosupported.In the 2X high-quality ("Better") graphics mode, the off-screen graphics aredrawn at 200% life size, resulting in improved resolution for mathematicallydefined QuickDraw objects such as ovals, rects, roundrects, etc., as well asfonts, provided large font sizes are installed in the current system.  The IWthen prints them using 2 times the number of printer pixels/inch, resulting inan image with the same measurements at double resolution.Thus, the meanings of the names in the Print Quality dialog item are as follows:Name				Meaning----				-------Text Mode		ASCII text only; with approximate font mappingFaster			Graphics, Drawn offscreen at 1X and printed unbold/bidirectionalStandard			Graphics; Drawn offscreen at 1X and printed bold/unidirectionalBest				Graphics; Drawn offscreen at 2X and printed bold/unidirectionalBecause the 2X resolution at 144 pixels/inch in the vertical direction can onlybe achieved by a method of interlaced printing (the minimum sheet feed distanceis half (1/144") of the spacing of the print head striker wires (1/72")), doubleprint passes and 1/144" reverse line feeds are performed in some cases.The "Faster" mode is the only 1X mode that is actually printed at 80x72 dpi; the"Standard" mode is actually printed at 160x72 dpi, so as to facilitate highhorizontal resolution in the halftone patterns.SPEED PERFORMANCE ENHANCEMENTSThe new "Faster" mode prints in unbold/bidirectional.  However, Standard is thedefault mode for user-expectation compatibility with the old IW driver.While converting pixels from the offscreen bitmap to printer-ready graphics data,a trivial-white-space elimination catalyst algorithm has been implemented.  Itattemps to determine the value of a 2-byte word full of white pixels, in order toskip over large white areas very quickly.  A very general-case version of thisalgorithm is in place in portrait mode and a limited version (with much moresevere restrictions, such as the white having to be in standard color tablelocations) is in place in landscape mode.For certain very special (but common) cases (640-mode portrait with hscaling=1),pure black/white pixels whose RGB values come from standard color table locationsare imaged especially quickly by bypassing color system conversion in an unwoundloop.Before the printer-ready pixel data is sent to the port driver, it is broken upinto chunks of zero, nonzero repeating, and nonzero nonrepeating head-fire bytegroups, and the printer's positioning, print-repeating-graphics, and print-non-repeating-graphics commands are issued accordingly.  This run-length-encodingscheme greatly improves speed performance while improving compactness of data tobe sent across communication channels or stored up in a background printingspooler.After the run-length-encoding scheme described above, the data is cached (inmost cases) before it is sent out to the current print manager port driver.It is cached to never send packets smaller than an equate-defined limit (ie, 32)and never larger than another equate-defined limit (510--dictated by appletalkmaximum packet size).DATA SPOOLING METHODOLOGYThe new psuedo-"virtual memory" spool method is used by both the Draft (text)and Standard/Better (graphics, or "spool") printing modes.For spool printing, memory is allocated to memory and then disk virtually,thus allowing arbitrary-length documents to be spooled, assuming enough diskspace is available, unlike the older ImageWriter driver. During the actualprinting, the band buffer size is also dynamically allocated; in many cases,only one band and banding pass is necessary.During print spooling, memory is allocated a block at a time, optionallycontrollable by setting pFileBlockSize in the print record.  When total memoryin the system becomes less than a certain threshold, optionally controllable bysetting pFileMemLimit in the print record, the entire spool file in memory isflushed to disk, and the memory space already allocated is subsequently used asa buffer which is repeatedly flushed to disk when it fills up.  Memory isallocated with a user ID whose "type" field is $1000.If spooling to disk becomes necessary, the default pathname, optionallycontrollable by modifying pFileName (which is set to long 0 by default)in the print record, is:*/SYSTEM/DRIVERS/TMP.XXXX		type=$00	auxtype=$0000 access=%Drb---WRwhere the trailing X's are replaced by a random hexadecimal number.  If sucha filename already exists, then a different random suffix is used.  If theuser supplies its own filename, then no such unique suffix addition is done;supplying one's own filename is not recommended due to possible filenamecollisions when using shared volumes.  When disk spool playback is finished,the file is deleted, whether or not it was supplied by the user.COMMAND-PERIOD/IDLE PROCEDUREThe internal Command-Period-abort idle procedure is always executed in additionto any user-installed idle procedure, unlike earlier drivers.NEW PRINT RECORD FIELDSSeveral new fields have been defined in the print record.  Most are set todefault (0) values during print record initialization (PrDefault); they maybe left alone or modified by the advanced user:New Field Name		Offset		Offset	Size		Description--------------		------		------	----		-----------internA				prStl+2		$1A		word		MasterSCB during PrValidatepFileBlockSize		printX+0		$64		long		Chunkiness of spool mallocspFileMemLimit		printX+4		$68		long		Spool min mem left in systempCTable				printX+8		$6C		long		Color table for imagingpSCBList				printX+12	$70		long		SCB list for imagingwQuality				printX+16	$74		word		Internal quality mode selectionwPaperCrWidth		printX+18	$76		word		Variable crWidth valuewResScaling			printX+20	$78		word		Blow-up amount for Better qualitypFileBlockSize and pFileMemLimit are discussed in the section about the dataspooling algorithm above.  internA is examined by PrPicFile during banding buffermemory allocation, for example.If pCTable and PSCBList are left alone, the PrPicFile call will print withthe current screen color table and SCB list.  Alternatively, the user maywrite their own pointers at these locations in the print record, so as toavoid having to change screen colors.  Note that this will affect the entiredocument being printed, however (unless they were changed in the recordedpicture definition by certain PicComments; see the section "COLOR CONTROL").The following table describes the behavior of wQuality, along with two otherdata in the print record which define the current print quality/method selection:Name			wDev "Better" (bit)	bJDocLoop (byte)	wQuality (word)	Internal code----			-------------------	----------------	---------------	-------------Draft Mode	%0							$00					0						0Faster		%0							$80					1						1Standard		%0							$80					2						2Better		%1							$80					3						3Since it appears that most applications expect the "crWidth" field, in theprint record at offset oprStl+ocrWidth, to contain a constant value of 960 forhistorical reasons, the new wPaperCrWidth field will contain actual carriagewidth information based on the current paper type in use.  One of the uses ofthis new field is to communicate from the metrics calculations to the imager inorder to inform the latter of where the right margin is for doing landscape, etc.,based on the width of the current paper type.The wResScaling is a constant which describes how much this printer driver scalesup the image (just prior to scaled-down printing at finer dot pitch) in order toobtain higher resolution.COLOR CONTROL WITHIN DOCUMENTThe SCBs/Color Tables used during the imaging of each page are taken from thecurrent screen values unless overridden by certain actions taken by the applica-tion.  None of these actions will ever affect the colors on the display screen.The colors used for imaging are taken from the following sources, starting withthe highest priority:		1. pCTable/pSCBList print record fields, if not set to 0 (see above)		2. PicComments (see below)		3. Current screen valuesSince PicComments are recorded as part of a picture definition, inserting thesePicComments provides a method of changing colors on the fly in a document.Following the convention that all grafport settings are reset to default valueswhen each _PrOpenPage is called, all colors are assumed to be the current screenvalues unless overridden by a PicComment unless overridden by field in the printrecord.Thus, for custom colors, the following PicComment should be made right after each_PrOpenPage call, before doing any drawing:		PICCOMMENT KIND=251 -- Fill A Color Table With New Values		version			WORD			set to 0		signature		WORD			set to $A55A		tableno			WORD			set to 0..15 -- the table to be modified		table				WORDS [16]	set to the new values for the color table		reserved			LONG			set to 0						In order to use these different colors, an application must also be able tomodify the SCB's for each line from their screen-default ($80 or $00) values.This can be accomplished by issuing another type of PicComment:		PICCOMMENT KIND=252 -- Change A (Range) Of SCB Values				version			WORD			set to 0		signature		WORD			set to $A55A		Y1					WORD			line no. of first SCB to change (0..rPage.Y2)		Y2					WORD			line no. of last SCB to change (0..rPage.Y2)		SCBvalue			WORD			scanline-control-byte		reserved			LONG			set to 0		The driver internally uses a PicComment of its own, to keep track of where anew page starts within the spooled data area filled with picture definitionsrepresenting a page each.  For reference only, it looks like this:								PICCOMMENT KIND=250 -- End Of Page Marker (Internal Driver Use Only)				version			WORD			set to 0		signature		WORD			set to $A55A